## Test files written this session

- `.worktrees/task-detail-and-dates/test/task-update.test.ts` — 39 cases in five
  `describe` blocks, one per covered AC:
  - **AC-1** — a single-field patch leaves title, description, both dates,
    priority, status, id and createdAt each individually asserted; the change is
    then re-read through `GET /api/tasks` so the assertion is on persistence,
    not on the response echo; 404 for an unknown id; 401 without the token.
  - **AC-2** — for `description`, `deadline` and `priority`, an omitted key
    leaves the value and the same key sent as `null` clears it (the two halves
    the PRD names as separate assertions); `scheduledDate` cleared separately;
    one body that clears one field while leaving another untouched; an empty
    body rejected; a non-object body rejected; and six malformed values
    (null/whitespace/non-string title, impossible `2026-02-31`, malformed
    `01/09/2026`, out-of-enum priority) each rejected with the stored row
    re-read to prove nothing was written.
  - **AC-3** — deadline→scheduled and scheduled→deadline both clear the other;
    a flip-flop sequence re-read from the stored row to prove both are never
    set at once; a body carrying both rejected with 400.
  - **AC-5** — `detached` is read from the stored row, not the DTO (the PRD
    keeps it off the wire): false before, true after a title edit, true after a
    priority edit, and the occurrence stays bound to its series
    (`seriesId`/`occurrenceDate` unchanged — detaching is not unlinking); a
    one-off Task stays undetached.
  - **AC-9** — each of the seven named non-editable fields plus `lifeAreaId`
    and an unknown key rejected with 400, with seven columns of the stored row
    re-asserted; a mixed body carrying one valid and one non-editable key
    rejected whole; and a `done` Task edited stays `done` (PRD Open Question 2).

## Why this suite is legitimately RED before implementation

Executed against the phase-1 worktree state: **37 failed | 2 passed (39)**. No
`PATCH` handler exists in `src/worker/routes/tasks.ts`, so Hono has no route to
match and every case asserting a 200/400 outcome fails.

The two passing cases are recorded honestly rather than counted as coverage:

- *"requires the token"* passes because the auth middleware runs before routing
  and answers 401 regardless of whether the route exists.
- *"answers 404 for a Task that does not exist"* passes for the **wrong reason**
  today — Hono returns 404 for the unmatched path, not because the handler
  looked the Task up. It will pass for the right reason once the route exists,
  and it is retained because the 404-on-missing-Task behaviour still needs
  asserting; it is simply not evidence of anything before implementation.

No test asserts on an invented method name, a private field, or a call count;
every assertion is on an observable HTTP status, a response field, or a stored
row. No mocks are used — the suite runs the real Worker inside workerd against
an ephemeral D1 built from `migrations/`, matching `test/tasks.test.ts`.

